This code depends critically on a JNI native library, build from
Fortran originals and c "glue", as per the Makefile.
Native libraries are built and supplied for 
1) Linux (intel 32 & 64 bit) 
2) Mac OSX (PPC & intel: 32 bit only), 
3) win32 via MinGW.

DO NOT rebuild unless you have to - it's a slightly delicate build.
See README_WINDOWS for windows building

(1) You need a current JDK to run the code.
Get a recent jdk (*NOT the jre*) from sun, e.g. browse around
http://java.sun.com/javase/downloads/index.jsp

Use at least Java 1.5
Do the standard install.

The code has been written to seek the correct library "directly* as a System.load(),
and not require the usual JAVA_LIBRARY_PATH mechanisms.

(2) An example script is provided as ../../../../../scripts/csem1Dinverter. 

a) OSX, Linux: his should work fine for Linux and OSX out of the box.
   On OSX: you *might* have to set your JVM to 32bit for now (tho' I think the JNI loading now fixes this)

b) Windows: 

Either minGW, (or cygwin), paths to java will have to be set in your
environment.
The script above is works fine in the cygwin environment. Small tweaks may be required
to get it to run as a DOS batch script. The critical requirement is to set the
environment variables

SEISMIC_INVERTER_HOME=directory at top of the delivery distribution (from this level, ../../../../..)
CSIRO_OSTYPE=cygwin

The script ../../../../../scripts/csem1Dinverter (and the included environment_setup.sh) should make
it obvious how these are used to derive a CLASSPATH for java.

The JNI library is loaded within the cygwin directory, so make sure you have set
CSIRO_OSTYPE=cygwin.

Clearly the script will vary depending on whther you use bash within cygwin,
or some direct DOS batch-script (in which I have no expertise).
